===Verb===
{{en-verb|||shook|n|past2=~d|past2_qual=rare|past3=shooketh|past3_qual=slang|past_ptc2=shook|past_ptc2_qual=dialectal}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|ergative}} To cause (something) to move rapidly in opposite directions alternatingly.
#: {{ux|en|The earthquake '''shook''' the building.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''shook''' the can of soda for thirty seconds before delivering it to me, so that, when I popped it open, soda went everywhere.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=Meeting Point|page=232|passage=Meanwhile Nanny Broome was recovering from her initial panic and now seemed anxious to make up for any kudos she might have lost, by exerting her personality to the utmost. She took the policeman's helmet and placed it on a chair, and unfolded his tunic to '''shake''' it and fold it up again for him.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To move (one's head) from side to side, especially to indicate [[refusal]], [[reluctance]]{{,}} or [[disapproval]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Shaking''' his head, he kept repeating “No, no, no”.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Psalms|109|25|page=705|column=1|passage=I became alſo a reproch vnto them: ''when'' they looked vpon me, they '''ſhaked''' their heads.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To move or remove by agitating; to throw off by a jolting or vibrating motion.
#: {{ux|en|to '''shake''' fruit down from a tree}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Pericles|act=III|scene=ii|pageref=40|passage={{...}}'''Shake''' off the golden ſlumber of repoſe;{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|edition=5th|page=70|passage=But indeed this ''Shame'' was a bold Villain; I could ſcarce '''ſhake''' him out of my company; [...]}}
# {{senseid|en|disturb emotionally}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[disturb]] emotionally; to [[shock]].
#: {{synonyms|en|traumatize}}
#: {{ux|en|Her father’s death '''shook''' her terribly.}}
#: {{ux|en|He was '''shaken''' by what had happened.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-07-20|volume=408|issue=8845|magazine=w:The Economist
